# Drafted by Gemini (R19 road), edited. The gate, the Corpse Road and King's Rest.

~ gate_arrive
set mm_gate_seen
cutscene mm_broken_gate
Narrator: A spiked fence of black cold iron runs off into the clammy fog on either hand, bitter to the touch. Before you, the gate hangs twisted off its hinges between two stone pillars carved like hooded reapers.
interject name:rook_coldwake: From a strictly municipal standpoint, this perimeter fencing represents a catastrophic failure of township maintenance and boundary compliance.
-> END

~ gate_chain
set mm_chain_seen
Narrator: Fifty feet of thick iron chain lies snapped in the black mud beside the gatepost. Its rusted links are wrenched open and bent outward toward the road.
* [Investigation DC 13] Study how the chain broke -> chain_clever | chain_plain
* Take the chain, it is iron and the shrouded fear iron -> chain_take
* Leave it -> END

~ chain_clever
Narrator: Looking closer at the sheared iron, you see the heavy links were burst outward from the cemetery side. Whatever broke through this gate was already locked inside the fence.
set mm_gate_burst_known
-> chain_take

~ chain_plain
Narrator: The broken iron is old and freezing to the touch, and it tells you nothing more than that.
-> chain_take

~ chain_take
give mm_iron_chain
Narrator: You coil fifty feet of freezing, mud-slick iron over a shoulder, and the dead weight of it feels strangely reassuring.
-> END

~ mile1
set mm_mile1_done
Narrator: In the first mile, the wet fog leans against your shoulders like sodden wool and every tilted headstone seems to turn its face toward you. A thin, reed-like wail sets in right behind your eyes.
check Wisdom DC 15 -> mile1_ok | mile1_fail

~ mile1_ok
damage 1d6 psychic party
Narrator: You grit your teeth against the ringing in your skulls and push through with nothing worse than a dull headache behind your brows.
-> END

~ mile1_fail
damage 2d6 psychic party
damage 2d6 necrotic party
Narrator: The keen of the dead bores through bone and ear alike, and the graveyard cold sinks into your chests until every one of you staggers in the mud.
-> END

~ mile3
set mm_mile3_done
Narrator: The third mile is the coldest of all, smelling of wet slate and old pennies. Your breath hangs white in the dead air and feels as though it never quite comes back out.
check Constitution DC 15 -> mile3_ok | mile3_fail

~ mile3_ok
Narrator: You keep your cloaks tight and march through the freezing mist, cresting the dark hilltop with breath and strength to spare.
-> END

~ mile3_fail
disadvantage checks 2h: the long cold walk
Narrator: The cemetery frost settles deep into the marrow of your bones, leaving a leaden ache that will not leave you for a good long while.
-> END

~ kings_rest
set mm_kings_rest_seen
Narrator: Before a pair of rune-scarred stone doors, armored wights and hollow-eyed shrouded hack at one another in the mist without end. Above the fray stands the weathered statue of a giant king, his broadsword raised to the moon.
if flag.yorgrim_with_party
yorgrim [afraid]: Step away from those carved stones if you value your breath. The wards on that threshold kill, and what sleeps behind the iron must never wake.
endif
Narrator: The great rune-carved doors do not budge a hair under your hands, and a cold prickle in your palms tells you it would be a grave mistake if they did.
-> END
